body:
## Symptom

A widget that starts the next async task from inside the previous task's completion callback (a plain "work queue" / pagination pattern) fails with

```
Stack overflow (line N, position M)
```

after a small, fixed number of hops, even though each callback itself is shallow. The failing operation is the completion callback (`execution.operation.callback`), and the view's `last_error` / diagnostic carries it.

Minimal repro (any async capability that completes quickly; here oh-my-byted's `omb.exec` running `true`):

```rhai
fn start_chain(ctx, deps) { hop(ctx); }
fn cleanup_chain(ctx, deps) { () }
fn hop(ctx) {
    ctx.start_task("omb.exec", "run", #{ program: "true", args: [], timeout_ms: 5000 },
        Fn("hopped"), Fn("failed"));
}
fn hopped(ctx, out) {
    let n = ctx.get_state("hops") + 1;
    ctx.set_state("hops", n);
    if n < 200 { hop(ctx); }
}
fn failed(ctx, error) { () }

fn render_Widget(ctx, props) {
    effect("chain", (), Fn("start_chain"), Fn("cleanup_chain"));
    text(`hops=${ctx.get_state("hops")}`)
}
```

Observed with gpui-rhai 0.1.5: the chain stops at `hops=13` with `Stack overflow (line 32, ...)` (the `ctx.start_task` line inside `hop`). Each hop adds roughly `hopped -> hop -> start_task` levels on top of the previous one, and `configure_engine_limits` sets `max_call_levels(64)`.

Real-world trigger: resolving ~40 upstream task names with a "finish one, start the next" worker pool died after ~37 completions.

## Root cause (from reading 0.1.5)

`ScriptInvocationContext::capture` (`src/invocation.rs`) stores `context.store_data()`, i.e. a `NativeCallContextStore` whose `global: GlobalRuntimeState` includes `level` — the call depth at the moment the callback was registered. When the host later dispatches the retained callback via `call_within_context`, Rhai resumes at that stored level. A callback that registers the next callback captures a deeper level again, so depth accumulates across hops that are not actually nested at runtime.

The operation counter already has the equivalent problem handled (`operation_base`, and the progress adapter's `align`); the call level does not.

## Suggested fix

When capturing a context for a *retained / delayed* invocation (task and subscription callbacks, event handlers), reset the stored depth, e.g.

```rust
let mut data = context.store_data();
data.global.level = 0; // delayed dispatch starts a fresh stack
```

(or record a `level_base` and subtract it, mirroring `operation_base`). Synchronous nested calls keep their real depth, so the recursion guard still works where it matters.

## Downstream workaround

oh-my-byted widgets queue the work in state and start each batch from an `effect` (effects are dispatched from the render cycle, so depth starts fresh); callbacks only bump a batch counter when a whole batch finishes.
